<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#natron">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Natron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Natron is a naturally occurring mixture of sodium salts, used historically in cleaning, preservation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">glassmaking</a> and medicine. Deposits in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> made it a trade good. It was among the commodities carried north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> along the Darb al-Arba'in, the forty-day caravan route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/natron.jpg" alt="Natron" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Thüngen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emi_Koussi_crater_natron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English and German word natron is a French cognate derived through the Spanish natrón from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> natrium and Greek nitron, which in turn derives from the Ancient Egyptian word nṯrj. It refers to Wadi El Natrun, or the Natron Valley, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, from which the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> mined natron for use in burial rites. The chemical symbol for sodium, Na, abbreviates the element's Neo-Latin name natrium, derived from natron. Nitrogen is also a cognate, through the Greek nitron. Niter is an obsolete name for natron, because in earlier times the two minerals were confused with each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Natron is a mixture of sodium carbonate decahydrate, a kind of soda ash, and around 17% sodium bicarbonate, with small quantities of sodium chloride and sodium sulfate. It is white to colourless when pure and gray or yellow with impurities. In modern mineralogy the term means only the sodium carbonate decahydrate that makes up most of the historical salt.</p><p>Natron forms as saline lakes dry up in arid environments. Pure deposits are rare because the decahydrate is stable only over a limited temperature range: it recrystallises at 32 °C to the heptahydrate and above 37 to 38 °C to the monohydrate. The mineral forms only if the brine stays at about 20 °C or below during evaporation, or up to about 30 °C where the lake is very alkaline. Sources include Wadi El Natrun, the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, Lake Natron in Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, and the Great Hungarian Plain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> natron was harvested directly from dry lake beds and used for thousands of years as a cleaning product for home and body. Blended with oil, it was an early soap. Undiluted, it cleaned the teeth and served as a mouthwash. It went into early antiseptics for wounds and was used to dry and preserve fish and meat, to kill household insects, to make leather and to bleach clothing. Added to castor oil, it made a smokeless fuel that let artisans paint inside tombs without staining them with soot.</p><p>In mummification natron acted as a drying agent because it absorbs water, and its carbonate raises alkalinity, which creates a hostile environment for bacteria. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> describe pellets of natron as funerary offerings, drawn from both Lower and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. Natron was an ingredient of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-blue">Egyptian blue</a> and the flux in Egyptian faience. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and others used it with sand and lime in ceramics and glass until at least 640 CE. Most of its uses were later taken over by soda ash and baking soda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Natron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
